<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cosimo I de' Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1519">1519</a>–<a href="/years/1574">1574</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cosimo I de' Medici became duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> at seventeen after the murder of his cousin Alessandro in 1537. He crushed the republican exiles at Montemurlo, conquered Siena in the 1550s with Spanish backing and built a centralised state, commissioning the Uffizi from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a>. In 1569 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> made him the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici.jpg" alt="Cosimo I de' Medici" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bronzino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agnolo_Bronzino_-_Cosimo_I_de%27_Medici_in_armour_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cosimo was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> on 12 June 1519, the son of the condottiere Ludovico de' Medici, known as Giovanni delle Bande Nere, and Maria Salviati. He came from a different branch of the family, descended from Giovanni il Popolano, and a successor was sought outside the senior branch because the only male child of Alessandro was illegitimate and four years old.</p><p>Until his accession Cosimo had lived only in Mugello and was almost unknown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. Many influential men favoured him in the hope of ruling through him, but the Florentine writer Benedetto Varchi observed that the innkeeper's reckoning was different from the glutton's: Cosimo proved strong-willed, astute and ambitious, and soon rejected the clause that entrusted much power to a Council of Forty-Eight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Toward the end of July 1537 exiles under Bernardo Salviati and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-strozzi">Piero Strozzi</a> marched into Tuscany. Alessandro Vitelli defeated them at Montemurlo and stormed the fortress, and the prominent prisoners were later beheaded. Cosimo won recognition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in exchange for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. This restored the power of the Medici, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-gastone-de-medici">Gian Gastone de' Medici</a> died in 1737, and helped free Tuscany from Imperial garrisons.</p><p>With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s support Cosimo defeated the Sienese at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marciano">Battle of Marciano</a> in 1554 and besieged their city, which fell on 17 April 1555 after 15 months, its population diminished from 40,000 to 8,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montalcino">Montalcino</a>, the last redoubt of Sienese independence, was annexed in 1559, and in 1569 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> elevated him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a>. He was an authoritarian ruler who kept a guard of Swiss mercenaries and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzino-de-medici">Lorenzino de' Medici</a> assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1548. He built fortresses, laid heavy taxes on his subjects and developed a navy, entrusted to the Knights of St Stephen, which took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cosimo created the Uffizi, or offices, to consolidate his administrative control of the committees, agencies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>'s republican past; it now houses a major collection of art. He finished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pitti-palace">Pitti Palace</a>, laid out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boboli-gardens">Boboli Gardens</a> and supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a>, Benvenuto Cellini, Pontormo and Bronzino. In 1539 he married Eleanor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, who died of malaria in 1562 with their sons Giovanni and Garzia. In his last ten years, struck by the deaths of two sons, he gave up active rule to his son and successor Francesco I and retired to the Villa di Castello. He died on 21 April 1574.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cosimo I de' Medici” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Alessandro was murdered by his cousin Lorenzino in January 1537, Florentine leaders chose <b>Cosimo de’ Medici</b>, a seventeen-year-old from a junior branch of the family.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Alessandro was murdered by his cousin Lorenzino in 1537, and the 17-year-old <b>Cosimo I de' Medici</b> was chosen to succeed him.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Duchy of Florence</a></li><li><blockquote>Its small size made it dependent on stronger powers: <b>Cosimo I de' Medici</b> built Portoferraio on Elba in 1548, and Spain later held Porto Longone.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-piombino">Principality of Piombino</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis between France and Spain in 1559 withdrew French support, and Montalcino surrendered to <b>Cosimo I de' Medici</b>, joining the future Grand Duchy of Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-siena-at-montalcino">Republic of Siena at Montalcino</a></li><li><blockquote>When Philip II of Spain granted the conquered Republic of Siena to <b>Cosimo I de' Medici</b> in 1557, he kept the coastal fortresses of Orbetello, Porto Ercole, Talamone, Porto Santo Stefano and Ansedonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-the-presidi">State of the Presidi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Villa Castello, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cosimo I de' Medici"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
